The logger_* and elog_shim programs test the hierarchical logger of
libgnuworld/logger.h. Every one of them links libgnuworld alone (no core,
no modules), which is itself part of what is being tested: the logger
knows nothing of IRC. Run under "make check"; each uses a local CHECK()
macro and returns the number of failures.

test_pushover_delivery is the one exception to "libgnuworld alone": it links
libnotifier and libcurl as well, and it is built only where libcurl is (see
test/docker/full-deps/run.sh, which is the build that has one).

test_logger_render
-------------------
The record types of LogRecord.h and the single-pass template renderer of
LogRender.h: positional and named placeholders, "{{"/"}}" escapes, that a
substituted value is never rescanned for further placeholders, unknown or
exhausted placeholders left verbatim, and the typed values (bool, signed
and unsigned integers, null) LogValue can hold.

test_logger_format
-------------------
The JSON, text and IRC formatters of LogFormat.h, checked against a
minimal strict JSON validator written into the test itself: key order and
typing of a JSON line, exact text-column output at INFO and at WARN
(including a truncated logger name and a two-line message), colour and
highlight on the console, and the IRC line's colour prefix, tag, function
prefix and bold spans, including that a value's own control bytes cannot
reach the console or an IRC line.

test_logger_sinks
-------------------
FileSink, ConsoleSink and CaptureSink of LogSinks.h: a file sink appends
rather than truncates, reopen() lands the next record in a new file after
the old one is renamed away, an unopenable path leaves the sink silently
closed, ConsoleSink::setEnabled(false) and Colour::No are honoured, and two
threads writing 1,000 records each into one FileSink produce 2,000 intact
lines.

test_logger_basic
-------------------
Logger itself: LOG_MSG with two extractors registered through
Logger::registerExtractor, a null object rendering "(null)",
removeExtractors() falling back to a raw pointer, sink thresholds, the
re-entrancy rule (a record logged from inside a sink's emit() reaching a
plain sink but not a suppressOnReentry one), the LoggerStream "write(level)
<< ... << endl" API MigrationChecker depends on, and a local notifier
subclass checking the "Class::method> text" / "text" (INFO) shape emit()
gives it.

test_logger_hierarchy
-----------------------
LogManager's registry: that get() creates missing ancestors and always
returns the same pointer for a name, level precedence (a configured level
beats a code default, which beats inheritance), additive dispatch stopping at a
logger with additivity = no, per-sink thresholds, moduleNameFromLibrary()
for a handful of real library names, and that the logger name column width
tracks the longest registered name, capped at 20.

test_logger_config
--------------------
LogConfig.h's parser and the configuration half of LogManager.h: a file
with sinks and logger lines parses into the expected structs; each class of
error (unknown prefix, unknown type or level, unknown sink id, a missing
required key, a duplicate logger line, a bad sink id) is rejected with the
whole file left unapplied; a missing file fails without exiting; configure()
builds every sink before it changes anything and keeps a code-attached
sink across a reload; and bin/logging.example.conf itself parses with zero
errors, its five sinks and its active cservice section included.

It also covers the settings of a kind of sink the parser does not know: that
they are collected into SinkSpec::options with the keys lower-cased and the
values trimmed, that the same settings on a file, console or irc sink are
still parse errors, that a repeated one is a duplicate, that "rate" is an irc
setting and nothing else's, and - in several broken files, with a
recognisable token in each - that NO error message ever quotes an option's
value.

test_logger_ratelimit
-----------------------
LogRateLimit.h, the token bucket a channel sink and a pager hold: that a
bucket starts full, that the refill is continuous rather than by windows
(checked with an injected clock, so the test neither sleeps nor flakes), that
capacity is the ceiling however long it stands idle, that a clock going
backwards changes nothing, that takeSuppressed() counts refusals and resets,
that parse() takes "10/min", "1/hour", "5 / sec" and "3/MIN" and refuses
"0/min", "-1/min", "10", "10/day", "x/min" and a count too large to hold, and
that four threads hammering admit() at one instant get exactly the capacity
between them.

test_pushover_delivery
------------------------
What a pushover sink of logging.conf really puts on the wire, against a local
HTTP endpoint (test/docker/full-deps/endpoint.py) that records every POST
body: one request per user key carrying the token, the title "[<logger>]
WARNING" and the sentence; a record of core.notifier NOT posted (the loop
guard); "rate = 2/min" posting two of ten records; a failing endpoint (/fail,
500) producing exactly one core.notifier ERROR and no crash; destroying a
sink with fifty requests queued behind a slow one (/slow) costing no time and
sending nothing afterwards; and no log record of the whole run carrying the
token or a user key. The sink is always built the real way, by
parseLogConfig() and LogManager::configure() from a file this test writes.

Without PUSHOVER_TEST_URL and PUSHOVER_TEST_CAPTURE in the environment it
says so and passes, so "make check" needs no endpoint and no network;
test/docker/full-deps/run.sh is what sets both.

test_elog_shim
----------------
libgnuworld/ELog.h: that every "elog << ... << endl" statement in the tree
still compiles and becomes one DEBUG record on the logger "legacy", one
line per std::endl, buffered per thread with a real stream's format state
(std::hex and the rest), two threads writing 500 lines each producing 1,000
intact records, and that setStream(nullptr) turns the console sink off.

test_logger_adversarial
--------------------------
Written from specs/2026-09-18-logger-core.md rather than from the
implementation: hostile field values (stray "{}"/"{x}"/"}}", newlines,
ANSI escapes, IRC control characters, a 10,000-character string, invalid
UTF-8, an embedded NUL) through render, JSON, text and IRC in turn, the
exact boundary of the coloured name column, a null pointer of a type with
no extractor, and 8 threads logging 2,000 records each while another calls
LogManager::configure() 200 times, checked for no crash and no torn line in
a file sink.
